                "content": "\\[\na+\\frac{4}{b}=b+\\frac{4}{c}=c+\\frac{4}{a}\\qquad (\\ast )\n\\]\n\nAll three fractions are defined, hence  \n\n\\[\na\\neq 0,\\;b\\neq 0,\\;c\\neq 0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Introduce the common value  \n\nPut  \n\n\\[\na+\\frac{4}{b}=b+\\frac{4}{c}=c+\\frac{4}{a}=k .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the first equality by \\(b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nab+4=kb \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ab=kb-4 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn the same way  \n\n\\[\nbc=kc-4 ,\\qquad ca=ka-4 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Equality of the products  \n\nFrom (1)\u2013(2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nab=bc=ca .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDenote this common product by \\(S\\;(=ab)\\).  \nUsing (1) we have  \n\n\\[\nS=kb-4\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=\\frac{S+4}{b}.\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, from the second equality in (2),\n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{S+4}{c},\\qquad \nk=\\frac{S+4}{a}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{S+4}{b}=\\frac{S+4}{c}=\\frac{S+4}{a}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Two possibilities  \n\nEither  \n\n* **(i)** \\(S+4=0\\); or  \n* **(ii)** the three denominators are equal, i.e. \\(a=b=c\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case (i): \\(S+4=0\\)\n\nThen \\(ab=bc=ca=-4\\). Multiplying these three equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n(ab)(bc)(ca)=(abc)^2=(-4)^3=-64,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible for real numbers because a square is never negative.  \nHence no real solution arises from this case.\n\n---\n\n#### Case (ii): \\(a=b=c\\)\n\nWrite \\(a=b=c=t\\;(t\\neq 0)\\). Substituting into \\((\\ast )\\),\n\n\\[\nt+\\frac{4}{t}=t+\\frac{4}{t},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an identity. Thus every non\u2011zero real number \\(t\\) gives a solution.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe only real triples \\((a,b,c)\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\na+\\frac{4}{b}=b+\\frac{4}{c}=c+\\frac{4}{a}\n\\]\n\nare t